From cadd95b3fd86011d0b11622679880ebd76ab4e8d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gigachad Enthusiast <32457258-AreteMaxxing@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 12:26:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Graphics paging: the window is what the camera shows

The window around the camera was fixed ranges, then a scale by
CyCamera GetZoom whose meaning was never known. What the screen shows
follows from the camera itself, so the window is now computed from it.

CvGame::graphicsWindowFromCamera reads the camera through
CvGameInterface.getCameraView: the eye and target in world units
(CyCamera GetCurrentPosition, GetLookAt), the screen's resolution and
globe view (CyEngine isGlobeviewUp). It casts the rays through the
corners and edge midpoints of the view onto the ground plane:
FIELD_OF_VIEW (the executable's own, read through
CvGlobals::getFIELD_OF_VIEW) vertically, times the aspect ratio
horizontally, which is the larger footprint of the two ways
FIELD_OF_VIEW can be read. A ray at or above the horizon reaches past
the map. The window is the footprint's extent west, east, north and
south of the look-at plot in plots (PLOT_SIZE world units each), plus
one, plus GRAPHICS_PAGING_MARGIN for paging out.

At 2200 units and 45 degrees of pitch on a 16:9 screen that is 15 plots
east and west, 12 north and 6 south; at 6000 units and 28 degrees it
reaches 83 east and west and 100 north, beyond the fixed 40.

- A side that grows applies at once; a smaller window applies after it
  has stayed smaller for GRAPHICS_PAGING_SHRINK_MS (5000), so zooming in
  and out does not rebuild.
- When the camera cannot be read, or the centre ray does not come down
  near the look-at plot, GRAPHICS_PAGING_RANGE_* is the window.
- In globe view the window stays as it is.
- The window is no longer symmetric east and west, so a turned camera
  is covered.
- GRAPHICS_PAGING_ZOOM and getCameraZoom are removed;
  GRAPHICS_PAGING_CAMERA (1) switches the camera window.

